
  [1mClassification: bounded.[0m The flow you want changed is already here in one file with one caller, so I'll present a
  short design in chat rather than write a spec. Say the word if you'd rather I go heavier.

  One thing I need from you before I touch the signature, because it decides the shape of the change:

  [1mThe fork:[0m a [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter means the [3mcaller[0m already knows the user's ID at the moment it calls [38;5;153mlogin()[39m. Right now
  the caller only has what the user typed into the form. So either you have an ID source I can't see, or the ID is
  something login should be handing [3mback[0m rather than taking [3min[0m.

  - [1mCaller supplies it[0m — [38;5;153mlogin(userId, username, password)[39m. Matches your request literally. Costs: the submit handler
    has to get a [38;5;153muserId[39m from somewhere (a new form field, localStorage, a tenant/config value). Cheap to change later,
    but it puts an unanswered "where from?" into the call site.
  - [1mLogin returns it[0m — signature unchanged; the result becomes [38;5;153m{ success, user, userId }[39m and the handler logs it. This
    is how "track who logged in" normally works: the server identifies the user during auth and tells you who it was.
    Costs: it is not the parameter you asked for, and any consumer of the return value sees a new field.
  - [1mBoth[0m — caller passes an optional correlation [38;5;153muserId[39m and the response carries the authoritative one. Most flexible,
    most surface area for a stub this small. Hardest of the three to unwind later.

  [1mMy recommendation: caller supplies it, as an explicit parameter[0m, only if you have a real source for the ID (an
  existing session, a tenant ID, a device ID). If "track who logged in" means "know which account authenticated," option
  2 is the correct design and I'd rather tell you that now than add a parameter the call site can't fill.
